  (W, "A value is hashable when Python can compute from it a number, its hash, that stays the same for as long as the value lives, and when two equal values always give the same number. Strings, numbers, Booleans and tuples of hashable items are hashable; lists, dictionaries and sets are not. The chapter puts the practical rule in one sentence: you cannot use a list or dictionary as the key in a dictionary, because these data types are unhashable, and if you need a list for a dictionary key, use a tuple instead [SW]."),
